- op: create  # 08d2
  at: '2026-07-31T15:25:06Z'  # 08d2
  by: agent  # 08d2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 08d2
  clock: 1  # 08d2
  nonce: 08d2  # 08d2
  data:  # 08d2
    kind: invariant  # 08d2
    title: "Member-name qualification vocabulary has two homes: type_hierarchy is blind to '::' so the framework-agnostic dispatch linker cannot fire for Rust"  # 08d2
    status: violated  # 08d2
    priority: 1  # 08d2
    parent:  # 08d2
    tags:  # 08d2
    - linker  # 08d2
    - protocol  # 08d2
    - linker_gap  # 08d2
    - dogfood  # 08d2
    isbefore: []  # 08d2
    duplicate_of: []  # 08d2
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 08d2
    pr_ref:  # 08d2
    description: "**The fact with two homes.** \"How a language qualifies a member name onto its\nowner\" is recorded independently in at least two linkers, and they disagree:\n\n* `linkers/containment.py` — `_SEPARATORS = (\"#\", \"::\", \".\")`\n* `linkers/type_hierarchy.py` — `_get_method_short_name` / `_get_class_name_from_method`\n  split on `#` (Ruby) and `.` (dotted) only. **No `::`.**\n\nMeasured, not inferred:\n\n    _get_method_short_name('Animal.speak')        -> 'speak'          owner='Animal'\n    _get_method_short_name('UserController#index')-> 'index'          owner='UserController'\n    _get_method_short_name('MyTrait::method')     -> 'MyTrait::method' owner=None   <-- BLIND\n    _get_method_short_name('MyStruct::method')    -> 'MyStruct::method' owner=None  <-- BLIND\n\n**Consequence.** `type_hierarchy` is the framework-agnostic (Protocol\nsubcategory) linker that emits `dispatches_to` from an interface/abstract\nmethod to its implementors' methods — the canonical **method -> method**\nanchoring used for every other language. Because it cannot parse `::`, it is\nstructurally incapable of firing for Rust, and for any other language whose\nanalyzer qualifies members with `::`. Verify the blast radius before scoping:\naudit which analyzers emit `::`-qualified member names (rust confirmed; check\ncpp, php static methods, scala, and any `Type::member` convention) and measure,\nper language, how many interface/trait dispatch edges `type_hierarchy` is\ncurrently failing to emit. Do NOT assume the answer is rust-only — that\nassumption is what produced the rust-specific workaround in the first place.\n\n**This is why `linkers/rust_trait_dispatch.py` exists.** It is a Rust-specific\nre-implementation (Framework subcategory) of what the framework-agnostic linker\nwould do if it could see Rust, and because trait members did not exist when it\nwas written it anchors dispatch at the **trait** (`MyTrait -> MyStruct::method`)\nrather than at the trait's requirement (`MyTrait::method -> MyStruct::method`).\nIts docstring states this explicitly and the statement is now false: \"hypergumbo's\nRust analyzer does not emit the trait's required-method list, so distinguishing\ntrait-required methods from inherent methods would require SCIP-level\ninformation.\" WI-duguk (`9baaa9afbd`) made the requirement list available.\n\n**Cost already paid.** That trait-level anchoring is what produced INV-fimon:\nonce WI-duguk rooted a trait over its own methods via `contains`, the\n`(trait, rust, method, rust)` partition carried two edge types and breached the\nG1 runtime-coherence ratchet. Ruled legitimate and allow-listed\n(ADR-0023 §3 amendment, 2026-07-31) — but the allow-list entry is only\nnecessary because of the anchoring, and re-anchoring would delete it.\n\n**Suggested shape (chokepoint, not per-site).** The separator vocabulary is one\nfact and should have one home — a shared helper both linkers call, rather than\na third copy. Then re-anchor (or retire) `rust_trait_dispatch` in favour of the\nframework-agnostic path.\n\n**Validation this needs, and why it is NOT a drive-by.** Both halves change\ndead-code reachability, which is a headline output:\n  * Teaching `type_hierarchy` `::` ADDS method->method dispatch edges for every\n    affected language.\n  * Re-anchoring rust dispatch REMOVES the trait-level edges. Reachability is\n    preserved only if forward-slice class expansion follows `contains` from a\n    `trait` node (`slice.py` has explicit class-expansion logic that excludes\n    `contains` from plain BFS — confirm it covers `kind=\"trait\"`, do not assume).\n  * Filtering to trait-required methods drops the linker's acknowledged\n    inherent-method false positives, but silently loses real edges wherever the\n    analyzer fails to emit a trait's members. Needs a fallback to the trait\n    anchor when the requirement list is empty (which keeps the ADR-0023\n    allow-list entry earning its keep rather than making it dead).\n\nCarry `awaits_bakeoff_validation` if any quantitative dead-code claim is made.\n\n**Related:** INV-fimon (the breach this produced) · WI-duguk (made the\nrequirement list available) · WI-kivut (built the rust-specific linker) ·\nADR-0023 §3 (the allow-list contract) · ADR-3bbb (Protocol vs Framework linker\nsubcategories — this is a Framework linker doing a Protocol linker's job)."  # 08d2
    fields:  # 08d2
      statement: "The rule for splitting a qualified member name into (owner, short name) has exactly one home, so every linker that needs it agrees; consequently linkers/type_hierarchy emits interface-method -> impl-method dispatches_to edges for every language whose analyzer qualifies members, including the :: convention used by Rust."  # 08d2
      root_cause: "The fact is recorded twice and the copies disagree. linkers/containment.py declares _SEPARATORS = (\"#\", \"::\", \".\") while linkers/type_hierarchy.py hand-rolls _get_method_short_name / _get_class_name_from_method over \"#\" and \".\" only. type_hierarchy therefore returns owner=None for every ::-qualified name and is structurally incapable of firing for Rust. linkers/rust_trait_dispatch.py exists as a Rust-specific workaround for that gap, and because trait members did not exist when it was written it anchors dispatch at the trait rather than at the trait method requirement -- which is what breached the G1 runtime-coherence ratchet in INV-fimon once WI-duguk started emitting trait members."  # 08d2
- op: discuss  # 2c0a
  at: '2026-07-31T17:53:46Z'  # 2c0a
  by: agent  # 2c0a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2c0a
  clock: 2  # 2c0a
  nonce: 2c0a  # 2c0a
  message: "SCOPE CORRECTION — this filing (mine, written this morning) is wrong on the same\naxis I documented WI-duguk being wrong on, four hours earlier. Every measurement\nin it is accurate; the generalization is not. The description told the next\nreader \"do NOT assume the answer is rust-only\" and then framed the whole item\naround the one cause that is rust-only.\n\nMEASURED. One interface + one implementing class per language, full pipeline,\njava as the positive control:\n\n  lang   method symbols                    dispatches_to\n  java   'Shape.area', 'Square.area'       method:Shape.area -> method:Square.area   OK\n  rust   'Shape::area', 'Square::area'     trait:Shape -> method:Square::area        type-anchored\n  php    'area', 'Square.area'             NONE\n  cpp    'Square::area'                    NONE\n\nThe '::' blindness is REAL and is rust's cause. It is NOT php's or cpp's.\n\nphp — three stacked defects, none of them the separator (php's Symbol.name uses\na DOT, not '::'; the '::' at php.py:344 is qualified_name, a different field):\n  1. `interface Shape` declared in the same file is emitted as\n     kind=\"external_symbol\", not kind=\"interface\" — there is no interface node\n     for the linker to key on.\n  2. Its method is emitted BARE (name='area', no owner qualification at all) and\n     rooted under the FILE, not the interface.\n  3. The relationship edge is `extends` -> external_symbol, not `implements`.\n\ncpp — a missing symbol, plus a deliberate suppression:\n  1. Abstract methods are not emitted at all. `virtual int area() = 0;` and\n     `virtual int perim();` both vanish; only `Square::area` (which has a body)\n     survives. So there is no parent method to dispatch FROM.\n  2. `modifiers` is EMPTY on every cpp symbol — no virtual/override tracking, so\n     the per-method virtual information that NO_VIRTUAL_EXTENDS_LANGUAGES defers\n     on does not exist to be consumed.\n  3. cpp is in NO_VIRTUAL_EXTENDS_LANGUAGES by design (WI-sukav A1), and\n     _extends_admits_dispatch only overrides for child_kind == \"interface\";\n     cpp's child is a class. This limb is CORRECT AS BUILT, not a defect.\n\nTHE RIGHT FRAME. type_hierarchy's interface->impl dispatch requires a four-link\nchain, and nothing measures the chain:\n\n  1. the interface/abstract type is emitted as a symbol            (php fails)\n  2. its method is emitted as a symbol                             (cpp fails)\n  3. both methods are owner-qualified in a separator the linker    (rust fails)\n     knows — containment knows (\"#\", \"::\", \".\"), type_hierarchy\n     knows (\"#\", \".\")\n  4. the relationship edge admits dispatch for that language       (cpp: by design)\n\nThree languages, three different links, one identical user-visible symptom: no\ninterface->impl dispatch edge. A separator fix alone moves rust and nothing else.\n\nREVISED SHAPE. The separator unification is still worth doing and is still the\nthrough-line (one fact — how a language qualifies a member onto its owner —\nrecorded in two linkers that disagree). But it is a LIMB, not the item. The item\nshould be a G2-style parity column, `emits_interface_dispatch` per language, so\nan empty cell means an analyzer gap rather than construct-absence, and each of\nthe three causes drains independently as a strict xfail. That is the same\nmethodology WI-rubip/WI-duguk landed for container members, and it is what makes\n\"do not assume rust-only\" enforceable instead of advisory.\n\nUNCHANGED. rust_trait_dispatch is still a Framework-subcategory linker doing a\nProtocol-subcategory linker's job, its docstring rationale is still superseded\n(fixed in PR #126), and the ADR-0023 allow-list entry for\n(trait,rust)->(method,rust) still exists only because of the type-anchoring."  # 2c0a
- op: discuss  # 2223
  at: '2026-07-31T20:34:14Z'  # 2223
  by: agent  # 2223
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2223
  clock: 3  # 2223
  nonce: '2223'  # 2223
  message: "PARTIALLY RESOLVED — the framework-agnostic linker now fires for Swift; rust's\ntype-anchored dispatch and the php/cpp links remain.\n\nThe concept audit that gated this (audit-findings 0018, PR #129) came back with\nthe OPPOSITE of what this item assumed, which is why the fix is not the one the\nitem proposed. `class` is NOT apex/peer overloaded — measured over 11 languages,\nevery analyzer emits it for a class DECLARATION and abstract-ness rides on\n`Symbol.modifiers`. So there was no fold, no ADR-0027 amendment, and no\n`stable_id` churn behind the v9/v10 scheme gate. All six values CANONICAL.\n\nThe defect is the missing PREDICATE, not the vocabulary. Landed in PR #130:\n\n  SymbolKindSpec.type_family          abstract_type | concrete_type\n  type_like_kind_names()              every nominal type declaration\n  abstract_type_kind_names()          interface / trait / protocol\n  is_abstract_type(kind, modifiers)   + `abstract class` via the modifier\n\nDeliberately NOT a new axis under ADR-0024: no IR field changes, no value\nreclassified, so nothing touches identity.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE (one interface + one implementor per language, full\npipeline, java as the positive control):\n\n  before   swift protocol  -> ['implements: class:Square -> protocol:Shape']\n           java  interface -> ['implements: ...', 'dispatches_to: method:Shape.area -> method:Square.area']\n\n  after    swift protocol  -> ['implements: ...', 'dispatches_to: method:Shape.area -> method:Square.area']\n           java  interface -> unchanged\n\nSwift gains interface dispatch for the first time. The gate was\n`child_kind == \"interface\"` — ONE of the three inherently-abstract kinds — so\nrust `trait` and swift `protocol` fell outside a rule `inheritance.py:91` got\nright 335 lines from a set that got it wrong.\n\nFour sites swept onto the resolver: type_hierarchy:493, inheritance:426,\n_transitive_bases:51 (a mutable-default literal, now a None sentinel resolved\nfrom the registry), cli.py:6629.\n\nA LINTER PREVENTS RECURRENCE, and its exemption rule is the part worth\nremembering. Naive \"enumerate all or none\" flagged eleven literals; only five\nare defects. `jackson_dispatch` and `kafka_streams_dispatch` declare\n`depends_on=[[\"java\"]]` and omit trait/protocol CORRECTLY — no Java symbol is\never either. `di_resolution` declares java/csharp/javascript/kotlin/python,\nnone of which have traits or protocols. So the linter reads each module's\ndeclared `depends_on` and only flags a missing kind a declared language can\nactually produce. Exemption two: an expression guarded by a sibling `language`\ncomparison (`s.language == \"graphql\" and s.kind in (...)`) is a per-language\npredicate that merely lives in core. Both are derived from data already in the\ntree — no allow-list with a justification field.\n\nNon-vacuity: planted-offender test plus one test per exemption, so a green live\ntree cannot pass because the walker is broken (L17).\n\nSTILL OPEN ON THIS ITEM — the other three links of the four-link chain:\n\n  1. rust  — dispatch is still anchored at the TYPE (`trait:Shape ->\n     method:Square::area`) rather than the requirement, because\n     linkers/rust_trait_dispatch predates trait-member emission. Re-anchoring\n     dissolves the ADR-0023 allow-list entry added by INV-fimon. The '::'\n     separator divergence (containment knows (\"#\",\"::\",\".\"), type_hierarchy\n     knows (\"#\",\".\")) is unfixed and is rust's specific cause.\n  2. php   — a same-file `interface` is emitted as kind=\"external_symbol\", its\n     method is emitted BARE (`area`, no owner) rooted under the FILE, and the\n     edge is `extends`->external rather than `implements`. Three defects, none\n     of them the separator.\n  3. cpp   — abstract methods are not emitted at all (`virtual int area() = 0;`\n     and `virtual int perim();` both vanish; only bodied methods survive), and\n     `modifiers` is empty on every cpp symbol. The NO_VIRTUAL_EXTENDS gate is\n     correct as built for cpp.\n\nAlso unfixed and filed on the audit: typescript emits `abstract class` with\n`modifiers=[]`, so `is_abstract_type` reads it as concrete. The predicate is\ncorrect as soon as the producer populates the modifier.\n\nThe audit's recommendation to reshape this item as a G2-style\n`emits_interface_dispatch` parity column still stands and is the right next\nstep — it makes \"don't assume rust-only\" enforceable rather than advisory, and\neach of the three remaining causes drains independently as a strict xfail."  # 2223
